  "title": "How AI Is Helping Engineers Improve Chips",
  "source": {
    "name": "OpenAI · B1 英文导读",
    "url": "https://openai.com/index/advancing-chip-design-with-our-models/",
    "published": "2026-11-18"
  },
  "level": "B1",
  "paragraphs": [
    "AI systems need computer chips to run. Now, according to a report from OpenAI, AI is helping engineers improve the designs of those chips. The company described work on its own chip, called Jalapeño, as well as a project with AMD. In both cases, the aim was to improve parts of a chip while keeping those parts able to do their original jobs.",
    "Engineers use code to describe how the parts of a chip work and how they pass information to one another. In the AMD project, OpenAI's model suggested changes to this code. Chip design software checked whether a part still did the same job after a change. The team also looked at how fast the part could work, how much power it needed, and how much space it took up on the chip.",
    "OpenAI reported improvements of up to 14 percent in its work on AMD's graphics chip design, compared with designs that engineers had already improved. AMD used some of the changes in a design it sent to be made. It saved other changes for future versions. The result came from this project, so it does not tell us how much a different chip might improve.",
    "OpenAI also tested models on two smaller processor designs. In these tests, a design that failed a check got no points. The researchers used a computer test to estimate how many instructions, or small steps, a working processor could complete in one second. OpenAI says it will work with Synopsys to train a new model to use chip design tools. The aim is to find more useful design ideas and check them with the tools engineers already use."
  ],
